"relationship": "related_topic",
"comparison": "Both disclosures address the classification and measurement of trading or market-related activity, though they focus on different aspects of performance attribution. JPM defines 'Loss days' to distinguish between total net revenue losses in its Markets business and the specific gains/losses used for backtesting VaR measures, noting that backtesting 'exclude[s] certain components of total net revenue.' Similarly, MS defines DARTs to specifically measure 'self-directed trades,' distinguishing this operational volume metric from the broader or discretionary trading activities that might be captured in other risk or revenue measures. Both companies engage in defining boundaries to ensure users do not conflate specific internal risk/operational metrics (VaR backtesting, self-directed volume) with overall business revenue or asset definitions.",
